    "body": "## Purpose\n\nSpeed up DeepSeek-V4.1 Engram lookups from CPU-offloaded tables. On GB200 they are bound by GPU address translation, not bandwidth: each row misses the TLB on the small pages that back the table.\n\n- **Sorted host lookups.** Large lookups from small-page host tables (pinned or `/dev/shm`) sort their rows by table offset, so neighboring rows share page walks. THP tables skip it, and so do lookups below the sort threshold.\n- **Inline big lookups.** From the inline threshold (tokens, max over DP ranks), the lookup runs on the main stream at full width instead of the prefetch stream. Persistent main-stream kernels (MegaMoE, mHC, block-scaled GEMMs) cannot co-reside with lookup CTAs, so a background lookup stalls them anyway, and EP collectives then wait on the slowest rank.\n\n`_engram_lookup_thresholds` picks both thresholds per GPU family: 36864 rows and 4096 tokens on Blackwell (measured on GB200). Other GPUs, including ROCm, keep both paths off and behave as before.\n\nAll Engram logic now lives in `common/engram.py`, so NVIDIA and AMD share one implementation and `nvidia/engram.py` is removed.\n\nAlso fixed along the way:\n\n- DP replicas share the tables only if `/dev/shm` has enough free space (it used to check the total size). The Engram DP leader decides and broadcasts, so replicas always agree, and peers do not hang if the space query raises.\n- The lookup kernel no longer ties the output row index to the id dtype, so int64 ids compile.\n\n## Kernel microbenchmark\n\nKernel only, single GB200 bound to its NUMA node, DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash table sizes (24 heads x 16M rows, 94.4 GiB), uniform random ids, CUPTI median of 3 runs rotating 16 id sets. Grid as in production: 76 CTAs below 4096 tokens, 152 from there. HEAD, new unsorted and new sorted outputs are bitwise identical, and from 64 tokens up the new kernel is never more than 1.5% slower than HEAD below the sort threshold.\n\nDEP2/DEP4 (24 heads per rank, `/dev/shm` table shared across DP replicas):\n\n| tokens/rank | before (us) | after (us) | speedup |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 64 | 90.1 | 88.1 | 1.02x |\n| 512 | 620.5 | 620.6 | 1.00x |\n| 1024 | 1236.0 | 1237.0 | 1.00x |\n| 2048 | 2463.7 | 2163.7 | 1.14x |\n| 4096 | 4621.3 | 3773.7 | 1.22x |\n| 8192 | 9243.6 | 5925.9 | 1.56x |\n| 16384 | 18476.0 | 8174.7 | 2.26x |\n| 32768 | 36963.3 | 9905.1 | 3.73x |\n\nTP2/TP4 (per-rank pinned tables):\n\n| tokens | TP2 before (us) | TP2 after (us) | TP4 before (us) | TP4 after (us) |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 4096 | 1791 | 834 | 440 | 447 |\n| 8192 | 3578 | 1846 | 877 | 160 |\n| 16384 | 7162 | 3457 | 1745 | 232 |\n| 32768 | 14319 | 4291 | 3481 | 396 |\n\n## Test Plan\n\n```bash\npytest tests/kernels/test_engram.py tests/distributed/test_engram_dp_shard.py -q\npre-commit run --files <changed files>\npre-commit run mypy-3.12 --files <changed files> --hook-stage manual\n```\n\nNew coverage: the sorted lookup reconstructs the checkpoint under TP padding; a `/dev/shm` that is too small or cannot be queried falls back to sharding, and the leader still broadcasts that decision.\n\n## Test Result\n\n117 passed on 4x GB200. Ruff, format and mypy pass.\n\n## E2E prefill (DEP4)\n\nDeepSeek-V4.1-Flash, DEP4 (DP4 x TP1 + EP) on one GB200 node: DeepGEMM mega MoE, FlashMLA mega attention, fp8 KV cache, `--max-num-batched-tokens 16384`, prefix caching off. `vllm bench serve` on random prompts with one output token, so every request is a single prefill. Base (5e887a078b) and this PR ran in the same job on the same node; each point is the median of 3 repeats with unique seeds after a warmup pass. No timed run saw a runtime compile.\n\n| prompt | concurrency | base TTFT p50 (ms) | PR TTFT p50 (ms) | speedup | base tok/s | PR tok/s |\n|---|---|---|---|---|---|---|\n| 4k | 1 | 126 | 128 | 0.99x | 31,961 | 32,060 |\n| 8k | 1 | 166 | 160 | 1.04x | 49,498 | 51,176 |\n| 16k | 1 | 321 | 295 | 1.09x | 51,265 | 55,732 |\n| 32k | 1 | 597 | 548 | 1.09x | 54,845 | 59,752 |\n| 4k | 64 | 1059 | 863 | 1.23x | 222,676 | 270,319 |\n| 8k | 64 | 2107 | 1721 | 1.22x | 236,670 | 282,490 |\n| 16k | 64 | 4239 | 3472 | 1.22x | 236,677 | 283,612 |\n| 32k | 64 | 8620 | 7056 | 1.22x | 233,837 | 284,409 |\n\nAt concurrency 64 every step prefills the 16384-token budget on all four ranks, so those rows share one step shape and differ in context length; their repeat spread is under 4%. At concurrency 1 the spread is up to 11%. Concurrency 4 and 16 are left out: their TTFT depends on arrival order and repeats spread up to 40%.\n\nWhere the time goes at concurrency 64 (estimated from the throughput and the microbenchmark, not profiled): a step takes about 280 ms on base and 230 ms with this PR. Sorting saves about 20 ms of it: each step looks up 16384 tokens x 24 heads in each of the two Engram layers, which drops from 18.5 ms to 8.2 ms per layer. The other ~30 ms comes from running those lookups inline, so they no longer stall the persistent MoE, mHC and GEMM kernels on every rank. At concurrency 1 only one rank is busy and the gain (about 24 ms per 16384-token step) is close to the sorting saving alone.\n\n## Accuracy (DEP4, this PR)\n\nGSM8K: 1319 problems, 5-shot completions, temperature 0, 4096-token cap, concurrency 32. Strict match 92.49% (flexible 92.49%), no request errors.\n\n## Not a duplicate\n\nSearched open PRs for Engram storage and lookup work. #58766 (FP8 rows into `wkv`) edits the same lookup kernel, #58310 (reject tables larger than host memory) adds a host memory check, and #57195 shares host tables across SP ranks. None sorts lookups or runs big lookups inline, but they touch the same files.\n\n## AI assistance\n\nWritten with AI assistance (Claude Code). The submitter reviewed every changed line and ran the tests above.\n\n\ud83e\udd16 Generated with [Claude Code](https://claude.com/claude-code)\n",
